text:
The review found 9 findings, all below. None is confirmed as a live data-loss bug in the tests. I ran `cargo test --lib daemon::`, `cargo test --test daemon` and `cargo test --lib cmd::request` on HEAD `00af08e8`, and all of them passed. I changed no files.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "src/daemon/server.rs",
    "line": 82,
    "summary": "The handler sets both signals back to SIG_DFL on the first signal, so a second signal from a different sender, not a second keypress by a person, kills the daemon in the middle of a write that the merge promises will finish.",
    "failure_scenario": "`moai daemon start` runs in the foreground under a wrapper that forwards signals (npm, foreman, concurrently, a shell `trap 'kill $pid' INT`). Ctrl-C sends SIGINT to the whole foreground process group, and the wrapper also sends SIGINT or SIGTERM to the daemon a few milliseconds later. The first signal starts the drain and the second hits SIG_DFL, so the process dies at once. The admitted write loses its journal line or leaves a temp file, the client gets no answer, and the socket stays behind. Commit 0df7b7d0 says a cut write on this path means decision B does not hold in real use, and commit 00af08e8 assumes a person presses twice knowingly. The cheaper fix is to escalate only after a time gap, or only on a second signal that arrives after a grace period."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/model.rs",
    "line": 1287,
    "summary": "Signals sent to the whole process group or cgroup also kill the `git` children of running requests, and `git_config` turns that failure into 'no identity', so a request in flight during a stop gets a false `no_actor` or a silently wrong read.",
    "failure_scenario": "The documented unit does not set KillMode, so `systemctl --user stop` uses the default `control-group` and sends SIGTERM to every process in the cgroup. Ctrl-C likewise reaches the whole foreground group, and `crate::git` does not move children to their own group (no `process_group` or `setsid`). A request with no `actor` is admitted just before the stop, and its `git config user.name` child dies. `.ok()?` returns None, so `model::actor` returns `NoActor::Unknown`. A write fails with `no_actor` even though identity is configured, which contradicts 'a running write still runs to the end' in docs/daemon.md:350. A read such as `ready` gets `Me = None`, sets nothing apart, and returns other people's rows as the caller's with exit 0 and no `partial`. The new test `a_write_waiting_on_the_lock_when_sigterm_arrives_still_finishes_and_answers` misses this: it calls `kill(pid)` on the daemon only and passes `actor`. Fix by putting git children in their own process group, or by adding `KillMode=mixed` to the documented unit."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/daemon/server.rs",
    "line": 97,
    "summary": "Only SIGINT and SIGTERM get the drain path; SIGHUP keeps its default action, so closing the terminal kills a foreground daemon in the middle of a write.",
    "failure_scenario": "A person runs `moai daemon start` in a terminal or an SSH session, as docs/daemon.md describes, and then closes the window or disconnects while a write is running. The kernel sends SIGHUP and the default action ends the process at once. The write can lose its journal line, the temp file stays, and the socket is left behind. The doc paragraph at docs/daemon.md:350 lists stop paths but never says that a hangup skips the 'write runs to the end' guarantee."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/side.rs",
    "line": 35,
    "summary": "`Side::ignore_env_actor` is a second name for 'inside a request', which `side::hung()` already answers, and the merge kept the flag, so one `Side` in the code resolves the actor differently from the rest.",
    "failure_scenario": "`request::answer` sets the flag, but `changes::notes` (src/daemon/changes.rs:264) hangs a Side without it. Any `model::actor` or `me()` call added under `notes` would read the daemon's MOAI_ACTOR, while `here_wanted`, `record_own_picks` and `add` on the same Side would treat it as a request. Every future `Side { .. }` must remember the field. The comments have also drifted. model.rs:1193 still says MOAI_ACTOR is skipped whenever a Side is hung. store.rs:288 says `here_wanted` uses the same rule as `actor`. daemon/read.rs:269 still says a dropped actor falls back to the daemon's MOAI_ACTOR. The test tail in `inner_omitted_actor` sets the flag by hand, so that part only checks itself. Simpler form: remove the field, have `model::actor` ask `side::hung()` as the branch did, and remove the exception from the `hung()` doc."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/daemon.md",
    "line": 344,
    "summary": "The doc says a request on an open connection after step 1 gets `stopping`, but a connection that has just finished a request returns as soon as it sees `stop`, so its next request gets a plain close with no answer.",
    "failure_scenario": "The merge makes the signal path set `stop` too, so this also applies to SIGTERM. A client sends write #1, the daemon gets SIGTERM, and write #1 finishes and is answered. `connection` (src/daemon/server.rs:531) then calls `wake` and returns. The client's next write on that connection gets EPIPE or EOF, not the `-32000 stopping` with its `id` that the doc promises. The doc mentions a no-answer close only for clients that connect at the moment of step 1, so a client written to the doc treats this as an unknown write result and not as a safe retry. Either read one more line and refuse it, or document this case."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/daemon/server.rs",
    "line": 62,
    "summary": "The comment says SA_RESTART stops in-flight socket reads and writes failing with EINTR, but this is false on Linux for sockets with SO_RCVTIMEO or SO_SNDTIMEO, and `rpc::read_line` does not retry `Interrupted`.",
    "failure_scenario": "`connection` sets both timeouts, and signal(7) lists those socket calls as never restarted. `rpc::read_line` uses `reader.fill_buf()?` with no retry on `ErrorKind::Interrupted`, and the `Err(_)` arm in `connection` and `lifecycle` then closes the connection silently. If the signal is handled on a connection thread, a half-read request is dropped with no `stopping` reply. Linux normally picks the main thread, so this is rare there. BSD and macOS thread choice is not pinned down, and that platform is in scope for the signal path. At minimum correct the comment, and retry `Interrupted` in `read_line`."
  },
  {
    "file": "CHANGELOG.md",
    "line": 18,
    "summary": "The merged signal entry says the daemon 'finishes the requests it is running', but reads are cut after 5 seconds, and no [Unreleased] entry mentions the socket-first stop, writes outliving the drain, or the new `stopping` error.",
    "failure_scenario": "A client author reads the release notes and assumes a long read survives a stop, but it is cut at DRAIN. A client also meets `data.code: stopping` with no notice in the changelog, even though the behaviour change (moai-3hgm.uz0) ships in this release. `grep stopping CHANGELOG.md` finds nothing relevant in [Unreleased]."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/daemon.md",
    "line": 350,
    "summary": "The paragraph names `launchctl stop` as a clean stop but gives only systemd's kill timeout, so launchd users are not told their shorter limit.",
    "failure_scenario": "launchd's default ExitTimeOut is 20 seconds, and the documented plist does not set it. A write still running 20 seconds after `launchctl stop` gets SIGKILL with the same consequences the paragraph lists for a second signal, but a reader takes the 90 seconds as the budget. Either state both values or set `ExitTimeOut` and `TimeoutStopSec` in the examples."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/daemon/changes.rs",
    "line": 244,
    "summary": "After the merge `poll` calls `lookup` and then `swap`, which searches for the same cursor again, and it ignores the `Option<Arc<Seen>>` that `swap` returns.",
    "failure_scenario": "Each poll takes the memory lock twice and scans the list for the same fingerprint twice. The `old` branch in `swap` and its doc ('finds the old snapshot the cursor pointed at') no longer serve the production path, only the tests that call `swap` directly. Simpler form: remove the `asked` parameter and the return value from `swap` so it only inserts, and let `lookup` alone touch `used`."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/daemon/server.rs",
    "line": 548,
    "summary": "`signal_drains_an_in_flight_response`, adapted in the merge, rebuilds its own server and client and puts its socket under `temp_dir()`, although the `Daemon` and `Wire` fixtures in the same module deliberately use short `/tmp` paths to stay under the 108-byte socket limit.",
    "failure_scenario": "With a long TMPDIR, such as a per-session Claude scratch directory, `<TMPDIR>/moai-signal-drain-<pid>/socket` passes the sockaddr_un limit and `bind(&path).unwrap()` panics before anything is tested. The test also repeats most of what `writes_outlive_the_drain(Daemon::signal)` now covers, and it leaks its directory on failure. Move it onto the fixtures by adding a `drain` argument to `Daemon::start`, or drop the overlap."
  }
]
